#!/bin/bash
# commit-msg — refuse a revert that does not say WHY.
#
# 2026-08-29. `git revert` writes:
#
#     Revert "corpus: the 2026-08-19 refresh, +5 chunks, finally committed"
#
#     This reverts commit 5440837a086666b2f2276bf1edd408bdf8c3860f.
#
# and that is ALL commit 3575779 said. Four days later nobody could answer why
# those 5 chunks were pulled — the KB drifted, a v113 build burned a red gate on
# it, and reconstructing the reason took an hour of forensics across three repos
# and four file states. One sentence at revert time would have cost ten seconds.
#
# A revert is the one commit where the diff CANNOT explain itself: it shows what
# was undone, never what was wrong with it. So this is the one place a message is
# load-bearing rather than courtesy.
#
# Narrow by design — it fires ONLY on git's own untouched template.
MSG_FILE="$1"
[ -f "$MSG_FILE" ] || exit 0

# Strip comments and blank lines; keep what the author actually wrote.
BODY="$(grep -v '^#' "$MSG_FILE" | sed '/^[[:space:]]*$/d')"
SUBJECT="$(printf '%s\n' "$BODY" | head -1)"

case "$SUBJECT" in
  Revert\ \"*) ;;
  *) exit 0 ;;                       # not a revert — nothing to say about it
esac

# Everything after the subject, minus git's own "This reverts commit <sha>." line.
REMAINDER="$(printf '%s\n' "$BODY" | tail -n +2 \
  | grep -viE '^[[:space:]]*This reverts commit [0-9a-f]{7,40}\.?[[:space:]]*$' \
  | sed '/^[[:space:]]*$/d')"

if [ -z "$REMAINDER" ]; then
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'

REFUSING THE REVERT — it does not say why.

  git's template tells the next reader WHAT was undone. It cannot tell them what
  was WRONG with it, because that information exists only in your head right now.

  This is not bureaucracy. Commit 3575779 reverted a KB corpus refresh with
  exactly this untouched template. Four days later the chat-bot KB was drifting
  in three directions, a v113 build failed a gate on it, and working out whether
  those chunks were removed deliberately or by accident took an hour across three
  repos. One sentence would have answered it.

  Add a line saying what was wrong. Examples:

    Revert "corpus: the 2026-08-19 refresh, +5 chunks"

    The release-note chunks need a dense embeddings rebuild to be usable, and
    shipping them without one kills the whole semantic leg silently.

  If the revert genuinely needs no reason, say that and it passes:
  "No reason needed - committed to the wrong branch."

MSG
  exit 1
fi
exit 0
